WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.710
The filmmakers behind Joker have been clear
that they never intended to adapt a specific

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:05.740
comic with the movie.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.180
Now that we can all see the film for ourselves,
it's obvious they meant it.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.160
Here are some of the biggest ways the Joker
of Joker is different from the comics.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.160
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.040
Throughout Joker, Arthur Fleck undoubtedly
lives in a world of fantasy.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.980
As he's watching a late night talk show with
his mother, Arthur imagines himself in the

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.420
audience, eventually being called up to the
stage by host Murray Franklin.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.560
He has an imaginary relationship with his
neighbor Sophie, thinking she's in love with

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.440
him when she barely remembers his name.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.990
These fantasies call every event in the film
into question, making it easy to argue that

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.980
the movie's climactic scenes are also a kind
of fantasy.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.530
It's possible to interpret most of the film
as simply Fleck's imagination, and that he's

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.180
always been a patient at the mental institution
we find him in at the end.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.640
While the Joker of the comics is certainly
no less prone to delusion, we're not seeing

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.980
DC Comics solely from his point of view.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.660
Joker is not just a violent fantasy he's real,
and his actions have consequences.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.340
While the Joker movie teases the possibility
of the plot being a grand delusion of Arthur's,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.840
the Joker of the comics is a problem for everybody.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.340
One of the more predictable events at the
end of Joker is the slaying of Bruce Wayne's

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:10.900
parents.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.070
As Gotham erupts in violence, the Waynes emerge
from a theater showing 1981's Zorro: The Gay

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.520
Blade, and Thomas Wayne steers them down an
alleyway in hopes of avoiding the riots.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.840
Unfortunately, one of the clown-mask-wearing
rioters spots the family and follows them.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.979
He calls out to Thomas Wayne and tells him
he's going to give him what he deserves.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:33.070
He kills the couple, yanking off Martha Wayne's
pearl necklace in the process, and leaves

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.580
the young Bruce Wayne helpless with his parents'
corpses.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.480
It's possible you've seen all of this before.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.700
"No.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.540
Stop.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:42.920
No!

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.940
Don't go in there…"

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.680
Even if we assume that we are meant to believe
the events of this scene actually do take

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:53.649
place and aren't just a part of Fleck's imagination,
it diverges significantly from the comic book

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:54.840
slayings of the Waynes.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.509
When Joe Chill kills Thomas and Martha Wayne
in the comics, there's no overt political

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:00.840
or social uprising at work.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.840
He doesn't wear a clown mask, and their deaths
have nothing to do with the Joker, who won't

00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:09.960
show up until 1940's Batman #1 after the Waynes
have been dead for years and Bruce Wayne has

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.560
already been crimefighting for a bit.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.440
The depiction of their killing that we see
in Joker is perhaps most similar to how it

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.840
unfolds in 1989's Batman; in that earlier
film, it's Jack Napier the movie's Joker who

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.240
proves to be the Waynes' murderer.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.500
We have no idea what the given name is of
the Joker of the comics.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.720
While it's revealed to be Arthur Fleck in
Joker and Jack Napier in 1989's Batman, in

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.820
the comics Joker has no legal I.D.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.300
We don't know if anyone knows his given name
we don't even know if Joker himself remembers,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.420
assuming he even has one.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:44.440
Some comics have suggested there is an undefined
supernatural element to the Joker, and that

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.500
he's been alive in Gotham for centuries.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.880
The closest thing Joker has to an origin story
is in the 1988 graphic novel Batman: The Killing

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.420
Joke, where we see flashbacks of Joker as
a failing stand-up comic who's lured into

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.771
a single night of crime to help support his
young wife and their unborn child, and it's

00:03:00.771 --> 00:03:04.790
that same night that finds him plummeting
into a vat of chemicals that changes his life

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:05.280
forever.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.640
Yet even in The Killing Joke, we never learn
the character's given name not even his first

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.140
name.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.600
As much as he hates his name, Arthur Fleck
should arguably feel lucky he even gets to

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.100
have one.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:20.500
As much as Todd Phillips insists Joker isn't
an adaptation from any comic, the story takes

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.920
at least partial inspiration from The Killing
Joke.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.800
In the graphic novel, we catch glimpses of
a possible origin story in flashbacks as Joker

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.350
works on his latest scheme.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.460
But even The Killing Joke is very different
from what we see in Joker and we all know

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.260
Joker prefers his backstory to be multiple
choice.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.320
"Y'know, it's funny.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.520
This reminds me of a joke."

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.400
In The Killing Joke, the young Joker is a
wannabe stand-up comic who, like Arthur Fleck,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.780
can't get audiences to laugh.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.270
Unlike Fleck, the young Joker of The Killing
Joke doesn't make up any fantasies that replace

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.120
his audiences' silence with laughter.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.700
He is painfully aware that he's a failure,
and with his wife expecting a baby he agrees

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.580
to join a group of crooks in robbing a chemical
factory.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.510
It's the crime that leads to the comic's fall
into a chemical vat, and his subsequent transformation

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.320
into the Joker.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.080
On the surface, at least, the fact that both
early versions of the Joker are stand-up comics

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.840
who aren't any good at comedy is where their
similarities end.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.620
Arthur Fleck doesn't have an unborn child
on the way.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.220
The only crimes he commits are ones he wants
to commit he doesn't let any mobsters strong-arm

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:21.800
him into it.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.340
And he also has nothing to do with the Red
Hood, the alias The Killing Joke's young comic

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.540
uses before his fateful transformation.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.860
The scenes in Joker of Arthur Fleck putting
on his makeup are some of the most quietly

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.060
chilling in the film, whether he's forcing
a smile onto his face so hard he tears up

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.280
from it or spreading the make-up onto his
tongue.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.400
But the Joker of the comics doesn't wear makeup
unless he's trying to look normal.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.800
There is no definitive word on how it happens,
but the artificial and clown-like attributes

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.120
of the Joker the bone-white skin, the green
hair, etc. are all typically permanent.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.520
Well, in Batman number 1, Joker does wear
make-up, but later origins reconned that out.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.960
In Batman: The Killing Joke, we get glimpses
of an origin story that shows Joker falling

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.080
into a vat of chemicals.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:07.440
In fact, earlier in the graphic novel, the
fact that Joker doesn't wear makeup is what

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.410
lets Batman know the villain has escaped Arkham
Asylum.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.840
He visits the facility to talk to Joker but
figures out Joker's escaped when he grabs

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.700
the man he believes to be the criminal by
the wrist and white makeup comes off on his

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:19.200
glove.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.860
Joker's origin story in The Killing Joke isn't
considered to be set in stone, but regardless,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.960
his skin, face, and hair will take more than
a shower to make it look like Arthur Fleck's.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.580
We don't see much of Arkham State Hospital
in Joker.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.680
Arthur visits it to get the file chronicling
his mother's stay there.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.260
From what little we see of the facility, it's
easy to spot fairly huge differences between

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.120
it and its comic book counterpart.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:45.800
The Arkham Asylum of the comics is a crazy
mix between Gothic architecture and space-age

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.420
security designed to hold killer clowns and
half-crocodile cannibals.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.760
It's usually depicted as being outside Gotham
City proper, behind intimidating wrought iron

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:55.260
gates.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.480
The guards are usually carrying enough high-tech
stuff to hold Darth Vader off for a few minutes,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.580
yet somehow Riddler and Joker and everyone
else constantly escape regardless.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.800
The Arkham State Hospital of Joker is anchored
more securely in the real world.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.840
Arthur gets there by bus and it appears to
be somewhere in Gotham City as opposed to

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:12.820
the outskirts.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.600
The interior doesn't look any more high-tech
than what you would expect in a 1980s hospital,

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.360
and when he goes to get the records for his
mom, Arthur appears to be in the same halls

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.540
as some of the patients, none of whom are
behind electrified bars guarded by guys with

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.180
more weapons than Judge Dredd.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.851
When Penny Fleck tells Arthur he's the son
of the famous Thomas Wayne, he goes to Wayne

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.420
Manor to confront his "father."

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.800
Instead he entertains the young Bruce Wayne
with magic tricks and comes close to choking

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.380
Alfred Pennyworth for claiming his mother
is delusional.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.340
Thomas Wayne tells Fleck the same thing when
Arthur corners him in a theater restroom,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:48.440
and Arthur's convinced his mother sold him
a fantasy when he steals Penny's records from

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.500
Arkham State Hospital.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:53.830
The file includes Arthur's adoption paperwork
and newspaper clippings about his abuse at

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.320
Penny's boyfriend's hands.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.800
The question is never completely settled.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.160
Arthur later finds a photo of his mother as
a young woman with "I love your smile" written

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.640
on the back and signed with the initials "T.W."

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.560
With Thomas Wayne's wealth and influence,
the notion that he could falsify records to

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.900
erase the stain of his mistress' child isn't
out of the question.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.990
In the comics, the notion that Joker and Batman
are blood related in any way isn't a thing,

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.530
though the question of Bruce Wayne having
a brother he doesn't know about has arisen

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:21.540
in recent years.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:27.340
As part of DC Comics' New 52 reboot, the "Court
of Owls" storyline in Batman includes Lincoln

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:31.860
March a mayoral candidate who claims to have
been born as Thomas Wayne, Jr. but Batman

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.220
refuses to believe him.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.340
Toward the end of Joker, Arthur's former co-workers
Randall and Gary show up at his apartment,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.080
offering spirits and companionship after his
mother's death.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.340
When it becomes clear Randall is only there
to fish for info on the subway killings, Arthur

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.880
brutally kills the old clown with a pair of
scissors.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.340
Gary cowers in a corner, sure that he's next,
but Arthur lets him go, telling him he's the

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.520
only one at Ha Ha's that was ever nice to
him.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.240
The comic book Joker has grown exceedingly
cruel and ruthless over the years, and leaving

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.320
survivors behind isn't one of his hobbies.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:05.940
The fact that Gary has purer intentions when
visiting Arthur or treated him "nice" wouldn't

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:06.960
change his fate.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.610
If anything, the fact that Gary had every
reason to expect mercy would serve as more

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:14.240
incentive for the comic book Joker to kill
him, because it would make the whole thing

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.760
funnier in his twisted mind.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.780
Perhaps one of the more obvious ways Joker's
Arthur Fleck differs from his comic book counterpart

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.560
is his lack of killer gadgets.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.360
No acid-shooting lapel flowers and no electrocuting
hand buzzers.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:30.280
The Joker of the comics is so heavily armed
with the stuff, he's essentially a walking

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:30.980
booby trap.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:36.120
But Arthur Fleck uses no weapons more creative
than a gun, a pair of scissors, and a pillow.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:39.480
Well go on, open it."

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.080
Of course, this makes sense.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:52.200
Arthur Fleck isn't in a world with Bat-themed
vigilantes or Legions of Doom at least, not

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:52.700
yet.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.140
Joker is much more grounded in the real world
than most Batman films, and filling it with

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.980
deadly gift-wrapped bombs or pistols that
shoot "BANG" flags wouldn't fit.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:05.760
The closest things to gadgets we see Arthur
use are his perfectly non-lethal clown props,

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.360
like the wand he uses to entertain the young
Bruce Wayne.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.380
Look closely after he gets beaten by teenagers
in the beginning of Joker and you can see

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:16.710
the water draining from his lapel flower onto
the concrete, giving the impression it's just

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.400
as precious to the clown as blood.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:23.160
In the comics, it's always either Batman or
one of his costumed allies who brings the

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.340
Joker to justice.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:27.760
The less "super" law enforcement of Gotham
really can't challenge him.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.140
"What is that?

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:33.880
What is that, a bazooka?"

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:42.040
Even when Batman catches the clown, he's destined
to escape, but at the very least it takes

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.720
someone with a mask to give Gotham a break
from Joker for a couple of days.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.720
Such is not the case in Joker.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:52.060
In fact, the Arthur Fleck of Joker not only
doesn't seem all that tough to apprehend,

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.660
he doesn't seem particularly concerned with
being caught.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.980
After he kills Murray Franklin, he apparently
just hangs around the studio until the police

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:02.490
come for him, because the next time we see
him he's being driven away in the back of

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:03.480
a police cruiser.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.680
So while the Gotham City of Joker certainly
could use some help with its garbage strike

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.860
and its impoverished citizens, they seem to
be able to catch Joker without any costumed

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:12.800
crimefighters' help.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.290
In this version of the story, Gotham needs
civic reforms way more than they need a dude

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.600
dressed like a bat who dropkicks muggers.

